    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="br-what">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split split--top">
          <div>
            <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Overview</p>
            <h2 class="title" id="br-what" data-fx="wipe">What is a <span class="grad-text">brain tumor?</span></h2>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Your brain controls everything you do: thinking, speaking, moving, seeing and breathing. A brain tumor forms when cells in or around the brain (such as the nerves, the pituitary gland or the coverings of the brain) grow out of control and form a mass. Both benign and malignant tumors can cause serious problems because the skull has no room to expand.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">A tumor can cause problems in two ways: by pressing on or damaging nearby brain tissue, and by blocking the flow of fluid around the brain, which raises pressure inside the skull. Even a benign brain tumor can be serious if it grows in an important area. That is why every brain tumor needs expert care.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Dr. Neema Bhat, a US-trained pediatric hematologist-oncologist with 10+ years of experience, works with neurosurgeons and radiation oncologists to plan complete brain tumor treatment in Bangalore, with special expertise in children.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-doc"/></svg>Two main groups</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Primary brain tumors</b> start in the brain or its surrounding tissues. They can be benign or malignant.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Secondary (metastatic) brain tumors</b> start in another part of the body, such as the lungs, breast or kidney, and spread to the brain. In adults, these are more common than primary brain tumors.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">India sees over 28,000 new brain tumor cases every year (IARC data). Brain tumors are also among the most common cancers in children, after leukemia and lymphoma. Early signs like headaches and vomiting are easy to ignore, so timely brain tumor treatment in Bangalore can protect brain function and quality of life.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="br-sym">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Signs &amp; symptoms</p><h2 class="title" id="br-sym" data-fx="wipe">Common signs of a <span class="grad-text">brain tumor</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Symptoms depend on the size and location of the tumor. Watch for:</p>
        </div><ul class="sym sym--3"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-brain"/></svg><b>Headaches</b><span>New, getting worse, or worse in the morning or when lying down</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg><b>Nausea or vomiting</b><span>Especially in the morning</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-brain"/></svg><b>Fits (seizures)</b><span>Especially for the first time in an adult</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-eye"/></svg><b>Vision problems</b><span>Blurred or double vision, or loss of side vision</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-activity"/></svg><b>Weakness or numbness</b><span>In an arm, leg or one side of the body</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-bone"/></svg><b>Balance problems</b><span>Trouble with walking or coordination</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-chat"/></svg><b>Speech problems</b><span>Trouble speaking or understanding words</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><b>Behaviour changes</b><span>Changes in memory, mood or personality</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-moon"/></svg><b>Unusual sleepiness</b><span>Or confusion</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><b>Hearing changes</b><span>Hearing loss or ringing in one ear</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><b>Hormone problems</b><span>Irregular periods, unusual growth or milk production (pituitary tumors)</span></li></ul><div class="cols2 mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg>Brain tumor symptoms in children</h3><p class="box__sub">Children may have morning headaches with vomiting, unsteady walking, a squint, a head tilt, delayed or lost milestones, early or delayed puberty, poor growth, or a drop in school performance. In babies, the head may grow too fast or the soft spot on the head may bulge. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US board-certified pediatric hematologist-oncologist, so children with brain tumors get specialised care.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>See a doctor urgently</h3><p class="box__sub">If you or your child has a sudden severe headache, repeated vomiting, a first fit, sudden weakness on one side, sudden loss of vision, or becomes very drowsy or hard to wake.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="br-cause">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="br-cause"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>What causes brain tumors and who is at risk?</h3><p class="box__sub">In most people, the exact cause is not known. It starts with changes (mutations) in the genes of brain cells. Brain tumors are not contagious, and current research has not shown that mobile phones cause them. The risk is higher for:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Age:</b> some types are more common in children, and others in adults over 50</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Radiation exposure:</b> previous radiation to the head, such as for an earlier cancer</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Inherited genetic conditions:</b> neurofibromatosis type 1 and 2, tuberous sclerosis, Li-Fraumeni syndrome, von Hippel-Lindau disease and Gorlin syndrome</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Family history of brain tumors</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Weak immune system:</b> such as HIV infection or after an organ transplant, which raises the risk of primary CNS lymphoma</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Other cancers:</b> lung, breast, kidney cancer and melanoma can spread to the brain</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Most people with these risk factors never develop a brain tumor, and most people with a brain tumor have no known risk factor.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>What happens if a brain tumor is left untreated?</h3><p class="box__sub">Because the skull cannot expand, even a small growing tumor can cause serious harm:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Raised pressure in the skull:</b> severe headaches, vomiting, vision loss and drowsiness, which can become life-threatening.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Hydrocephalus:</b> a blocked flow of brain fluid causes fluid build-up, which may need emergency treatment.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Permanent nerve damage:</b> loss of vision, hearing, speech, movement or memory.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Uncontrolled seizures that affect daily life and safety.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Hormone problems:</b> pituitary and some childhood tumors can affect growth, puberty, thyroid and other hormones.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Spread within the brain and spine:</b> some malignant tumors, like medulloblastoma, can spread through the brain fluid.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Fewer treatment choices:</b> larger tumors are harder to remove safely.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">The good news: many brain tumors can be removed completely or controlled for years when they are treated early by an experienced team.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="br-types">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head head--center">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Types</p><h2 class="title" id="br-types" data-fx="wipe">Types of brain tumors <span class="grad-text">we treat</span></h2></div>
          
        </div><div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Type of brain tumor</th><th>What it means in simple words</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Glioma (astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Tumors from the brain’s support cells (glial cells). They can be slow- or fast-growing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Glioblastoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The most aggressive type of glioma, mostly in adults. Needs surgery, radiation and chemotherapy.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Pilocytic astrocytoma and other low-grade gliomas</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Slow-growing tumors, common in children. Often cured with surgery alone.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Medulloblastoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The most common malignant brain tumor in children. Starts at the back of the brain (cerebellum). Many children are cured.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Ependymoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A tumor from the cells lining the fluid spaces of the brain and spinal cord.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Diffuse midline glioma (including DIPG)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A fast-growing tumor in the brainstem or midline of the brain, mostly in children.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Craniopharyngioma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A benign tumor near the pituitary gland that can affect vision, growth and hormones.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Germ cell tumors</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Tumors in the middle of the brain, mostly in children and young adults. Many respond very well to treatment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumor (AT/RT)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A rare, fast-growing tumor in very young children.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Meningioma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Usually a benign tumor from the brain’s covering (meninges). The most common primary brain tumor in adults.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Pituitary tumors</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Mostly benign tumors of the pituitary gland that can affect hormones and vision.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A benign tumor on the hearing and balance nerve.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Primary CNS lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words"><a href="/lymphoma-treatment">Lymphoma</a> that starts in the brain, spinal cord or eyes. Treated mainly with chemotherapy.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Brain metastases</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Cancer that has spread to the brain from another organ.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of brain tumor"><b>Spinal cord tumors</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Tumors in or around the spinal cord that can cause back pain, weakness or numbness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="split split--top mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-award"/></svg>Grades of brain tumors</h3><p class="box__sub">Doctors grade brain tumors from 1 to 4 using the World Health Organization (WHO) classification, based on how the cells look and their genetic changes.</p><p class="box__sub">Today, genetic (molecular) tests on the tumor are as important as the grade, because they help choose the most effective treatment.</p></div>
          <div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Grade</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Grade"><b>Grade 1</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Slow-growing, least aggressive. Often curable with surgery.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Grade"><b>Grade 2</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Slow-growing but can come back or become higher grade over time.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Grade"><b>Grade 3</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Malignant, grows faster and spreads into nearby brain tissue.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Grade"><b>Grade 4</b></td><td data-label="What it means">The most aggressive, fast-growing tumors, such as glioblastoma and medulloblastoma.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="br-dx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Diagnosis</p><h2 class="title" id="br-dx" data-fx="wipe">How is a brain tumor <span class="grad-text">diagnosed?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Diagnosis needs both imaging and a tissue sample in most cases. Tests may include:</p>
        </div><ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Neurological examination</b><p>Checks vision, hearing, balance, strength, reflexes and thinking.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>MRI of the brain with contrast</b><p>The main test to see the size, location and features of the tumor.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>Advanced MRI</b><p>MR spectroscopy, perfusion MRI and functional MRI, to study the tumor and plan safe surgery.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>CT scan</b><p>Useful in emergencies, and to look for bleeding, swelling or calcium in the tumor.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">05</span><div><b>MRI of the whole spine</b><p>For tumors that can spread through the brain fluid, such as medulloblastoma.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">06</span><div><b>PET-CT scan</b><p>In selected cases, such as lymphoma or to look for a cancer elsewhere in the body.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">07</span><div><b>Biopsy or surgery</b><p>A tissue sample is taken to confirm the diagnosis and grade.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">08</span><div><b>Molecular and genetic tests</b><p>Such as IDH, 1p/19q, MGMT, BRAF and H3 K27M, which classify the tumor and guide treatment.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">09</span><div><b>Lumbar puncture</b><p>Checks the brain fluid for tumor cells in some tumor types.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">10</span><div><b>Tumor markers</b><p>AFP and beta-hCG in blood and brain fluid for germ cell tumors.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">11</span><div><b>Eye and visual field tests</b><p>For tumors near the optic nerves or pituitary gland.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">12</span><div><b>Hormone tests</b><p>For pituitary tumors and tumors near the hypothalamus.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">13</span><div><b>Hearing, heart and kidney tests</b><p>Before chemotherapy or radiation, as a baseline.</p></div></li></ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="br-tx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Treatment</p><h2 class="title" id="br-tx" data-fx="wipe">Brain tumor treatment <span class="grad-text">options</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Treatment depends on the type, grade, size and location of the tumor, its genetic features, and the patient’s age and overall health. Most patients are treated by a team of neurosurgeons, radiation oncologists and medical or pediatric oncologists.</p>
        </div><div class="cards3"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><h3>Active monitoring</h3><p>Regular MRI scans for small, slow-growing benign tumors that cause no symptoms.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-stetho"/></svg><h3>Surgery (craniotomy)</h3><p>To remove as much of the tumor as is safely possible, using neuronavigation and intraoperative monitoring to protect healthy brain.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-brain"/></svg><h3>Awake brain surgery</h3><p>For tumors near areas that control speech or movement, in selected adults.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-stetho"/></svg><h3>Endoscopic surgery</h3><p>Minimally invasive surgery, including surgery through the nose for pituitary and skull base tumors.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-micro"/></svg><h3>Stereotactic biopsy</h3><p>A precise needle biopsy for deep tumors that cannot be removed safely.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-radiation"/></svg><h3>Radiation therapy</h3><p>Precise techniques such as IMRT and image-guided radiation to target the tumor and spare healthy tissue.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-radiation"/></svg><h3>Stereotactic radiosurgery</h3><p>A single or few high-dose, highly focused radiation treatments for small tumors and brain metastases.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-radiation"/></svg><h3>Craniospinal radiation</h3><p>Radiation to the whole brain and spine for tumors that spread through the brain fluid, such as medulloblastoma.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg><h3>Proton beam therapy</h3><p>For selected children and young adults, to reduce long-term side effects, where suitable and available.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Chemotherapy</h3><p>Anti-cancer medicines given by mouth, into a vein, or into the brain fluid, often with or after surgery and radiation.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>High-dose chemotherapy with stem cell rescue</h3><p>For selected high-risk childhood brain tumors, such as some medulloblastomas, AT/RT and germ cell tumors, and to avoid radiation in very young children. <a href="/bone-marrow-transplant">Learn more</a></p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Targeted therapy</h3><p>Medicines chosen based on genetic changes in the tumor, such as BRAF, IDH or NTRK changes.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>Immunotherapy &amp; clinical trials</h3><p>For selected tumors, where suitable and available.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Steroids</h3><p>To reduce swelling around the tumor and ease symptoms.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-brain"/></svg><h3>Anti-seizure medicines</h3><p>To prevent and control fits.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-stetho"/></svg><h3>Shunt or endoscopic surgery for hydrocephalus</h3><p>To drain or bypass blocked brain fluid.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-scale"/></svg><h3>Hormone replacement</h3><p>For patients with pituitary or hypothalamic hormone problems.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-adult"/></svg><h3>Rehabilitation</h3><p>Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech therapy to regain function.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><h3>Neuropsychology and school support</h3><p>To help with memory, learning and returning to school or work.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-chat"/></svg><h3>Counselling &amp; palliative care</h3><p>Emotional support and symptom control for patients and families, at any stage of treatment.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><h3>Long-term follow-up</h3><p>Regular MRI scans and checks for late effects on growth, hormones, hearing and learning.</p></div></div><p class="alert alert--warn" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><span><b>Brain tumor care works best when planned together from the start.</b> Surgery, radiation and medical treatment should be coordinated by one team.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="br-life">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="br-life"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-sun"/></svg>Living well and recovery after brain tumor treatment</h3><p class="box__sub">Recovery is different for every patient. These steps help:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Take medicines exactly as advised, especially steroids and anti-seizure medicines. Never stop them suddenly.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Know seizure first aid:</b> turn the person on their side, keep them safe, and seek help if a fit lasts more than 5 minutes.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Do not drive until your doctor says it is safe, especially after a seizure or brain surgery.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Keep up with rehabilitation:</b> physiotherapy, speech therapy and occupational therapy work best when done regularly.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Prevent infections during chemotherapy:</b> wash hands often, avoid crowds when counts are low, and get urgent help for a fever of 100.4°F (38°C) or more.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Eat well and stay active:</b> freshly cooked food, enough protein and gentle walking help rebuild strength.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Support children’s learning:</b> tell the school about the child’s treatment, and ask about extra time or learning support if needed.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Look after emotional health:</b> mood changes are common. Talk to your care team or a counsellor.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Keep every follow-up MRI and visit, even when you feel well. Early detection of any regrowth gives more treatment choices.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Brain tumors usually cannot be prevented. But new or worsening headaches with vomiting, a first fit or changes in vision or walking should always be checked early.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

