          <h2 class="title" id="fq-title">FAQs</h2>
          <p class="lead">Quick answers to the questions patients and families ask most about brain tumors.</p>
          <div class="faq2__help">
            <b>Still have a question?</b>
            <span>Talk to the clinic directly.</span>
            <div class="faq2__acts">
              <a class="btn btn--grad btn--sm" href="https://wa.me/917899756677" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-wa"/></svg>WhatsApp</a>
              <a class="btn btn--line btn--sm" href="tel:+917899756677"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-phone"/></svg>Call</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          
        </div>
        <div class="faq2__list"><details class="qa" open><summary><span class="qa__n">01</span><span class="qa__q">Which doctor should I see for a brain tumor in Bangalore?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Brain tumors are treated by a team. A neurosurgeon performs surgery, a radiation oncologist gives radiation, and a medical or pediatric oncologist gives chemotherapy and other medical treatment. For children, a pediatric oncologist like Dr. Neema Bhat coordinates the overall care.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">02</span><span class="qa__q">Are all brain tumors cancer?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Many brain tumors, such as most meningiomas and pituitary tumors, are benign. But even benign tumors can cause serious problems because of where they grow, so they still need expert care.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">03</span><span class="qa__q">Is a brain tumor curable?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Many are. Benign and low-grade tumors can often be cured with surgery. Some childhood tumors, like medulloblastoma and germ cell tumors, have good cure rates with combined treatment. Aggressive tumors like glioblastoma are harder to cure, but treatment can control them and improve quality of life.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">04</span><span class="qa__q">What are the early signs of a brain tumor?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Common early signs are new or worsening headaches (often in the morning), vomiting, a first seizure, vision changes, weakness on one side, and changes in behaviour or balance.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">05</span><span class="qa__q">Is every headache a sign of a brain tumor?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Most headaches are caused by tension, migraine, sinus problems or eye strain. See a doctor if a headache is new and keeps getting worse, wakes you from sleep, or comes with vomiting, fits, vision problems or weakness.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">06</span><span class="qa__q">Does every brain tumor need surgery?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Some small benign tumors are only monitored, some are treated with radiosurgery, and some, like germ cell tumors and CNS lymphoma, are treated mainly with chemotherapy and radiation.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">07</span><span class="qa__q">What is the most common brain tumor in children?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Low-grade gliomas, such as pilocytic astrocytoma, are the most common. Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant brain tumor in children.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">08</span><span class="qa__q">Can a brain tumor come back after treatment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes, some tumors can come back, especially higher-grade tumors. Regular follow-up MRI scans help find any regrowth early, when more treatment choices are available.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">09</span><span class="qa__q">What are the side effects of brain tumor treatment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Side effects depend on the treatment and include tiredness, hair loss, nausea, and a higher infection risk with chemotherapy. In children, treatment can affect growth, hormones, hearing and learning, so long-term follow-up is important.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">10</span><span class="qa__q">Will my child be able to go back to school?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Most children return to school after or even during treatment. Some may need extra learning support, physiotherapy or speech therapy, which the care team helps arrange.</p></details></div>
      </div>
